  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 154 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nYet another day where fog is the only main forecast concern. This \nmorning's fog forecast is more challenging than yesterday because \nwinds have veered to a more southerly direction and we lost \ngood Lake Erie contribution. Still, excellent radiational \ncooling conditions exist with the loss of any upper level clouds\nand some moisture appears to still be bleeding into northwest \nOH from the lake. Guidance continues to show a narrow swath of \ndense fog developing along the Maumee River with some \nobservational evidence starting to support this idea. Confidence\nin widespread dense fog is much lower this morning and for now \nseems to be more of a localized, SPS-level event. Will continue \nto monitor obs closely through the morning and consider a dense \nfog advisory for the Fort Wayne to Wauseon corridor if necessary.\n\nLack of widespread fog and some light southerly flow/WAA today \nshould push highs into the low 70s for most with some mid 70s \npossible in the far SW. Afternoon RH values drop to around 35 \npercent and dry fine fuels will continue to support some concern for \nsmall field fires (limited by very light winds). Winds veer even \nmore for Monday morning and while some patchy fog may still be \npossible given light winds, not expecting any widespread/dense fog \nat this time, especially as crossover temps may be harder to exceed \nfor Monday morning. A weak front washes out across our north on \nMonday and a brief return to cool NE flow arrives Tue morning. This \nwill be another day to watch for possible fog development given ENE \nflow over the very warm lakes. Light S/SW flow returns Wed and Thu \nahead of the next front and highs surge well into the 70s (possibly \nnear 80 far SW). This front also washes out across our north as\nparent trough lifts into Quebec and ridge holds firm over the \nOhio Valley. A few sprinkles are possible in our far NW on Thu \nbut chances are low and impacts nil. SW flow quickly recharges \nheading into Halloween with highs well into the 70s and mainly \ndry conditions. Will have to watch the next decent system \narriving Sat night into Sunday for more widespread rain chances.\nStill plenty of time for subtle changes but for now best \nchances for rain appear later Saturday night into Sunday.",
